{"timestamp": "2026-09-23T13:45:58.821626", "data": {"url": "https://neuralcoretech.com/local-ai-self-hosted-llms-2026/", "title": "Local AI & Self-Hosted LLMs in 2026: The Verified Deployment Guide - NeuralCoreTech", "content": "Search SUBSCRIBE Search Search Search 2026--All Right Reserved. NeuralCoreTech Team Home \u00bb AI Hardware and Infrastructure \u00bb Local AI & Self-Hosted LLMs \u00bb Local AI & Self-Hosted LLMs in 2026: The Verified Deployment Guide AI Hardware and Infrastructure Local AI & Self-Hosted LLMs Local AI & Self-Hosted LLMs in 2026: The Verified Deployment Guide A practical, data-driven breakdown of the local AI stack, open-weight models, and deployment architectures shaping self-hosted LLM infrastructure in 2026 by Loucas Protopappas 29 March 2026 29 March 2026 2 comments 4 Facebook Twitter Pinterest Linkedin Reddit Email 1K Table of Contents The numbers tell a clear story. Ollama crossed 52 million monthly downloads in Q1 2026 \u2014 a 520\u00d7 increase from 100K monthly downloads in Q1 2023. HuggingFace now hosts over 135,000 GGUF-formatted models optimized for local inference, up from roughly 200 three years ago. The llama.cpp project \u2014 the C++ inference engine that powers most of the local AI ecosystem \u2014 has surpassed 99,600 GitHub stars . And OpenAI, the dominant closed-model provider, released its first open-weight models since GPT-2: gpt-oss-120b and gpt-oss-20b , both under an Apache 2.0 license. Local AI is no longer a niche experiment for researchers with spare GPU racks. It is production infrastructure. \u2713 Source: DEV Community / Pooya Golchian \u2014 Local AI in 2026, March 27 2026 This guide provides a technically accurate breakdown of the local AI stack as of March 2026. Every statistic is drawn from a verifiable source \u2014 primary documentation, official model cards, or independently benchmarked third-party reports. Where data is uncertain or model-specific, we say so explicitly. If you are exploring the AI Hardware & Infrastructure space, this article gives you a factual baseline for deployment decisions. Why 2026 Is the Practical Tipping Point for Local AI The Privacy and Compliance Driver Every prompt sent to a cloud LLM API touches external servers. For healthcare providers under HIPAA, legal teams handling client matter data, financial institutions under GDPR, or developers working with proprietary source code, this is a structural problem that contractual data processing agreements only partially address. Self-hosting eliminates the data transfer entirely \u2014 the model weights run on hardware the deploying organization controls, with no outbound connections after the initial model download. Kong\u2019s 2025 Enterprise AI report placed data privacy and security as the primary barrier to LLM adoption among enterprise organizations. This adoption barrier is the direct business case for local AI, and it is why NeuralCoreTech\u2019s coverage of Edge & Personal AI Hardware: Building High-Performance Clusters has consistently been one of the site\u2019s highest-traffic articles. Model Quality Has Crossed a Usable Threshold The DEV Community benchmark (March 27, 2026) found that local inference on consumer hardware now delivers 70\u201385% of frontier model quality at zero marginal cost per request on most production tasks. This figure reflects real measured performance on development workflows \u2014 code generation, summarization, RAG retrieval, and structured outputs \u2014 not synthetic benchmarks. More specifically: on HumanEval (code generation), Qwen 2.5 32B scores 83.2% MMLU , approaching GPT-4\u2019s reported 86.4%, while running on consumer hardware at no per-token cost. The compact Qwen3.5-9B \u2014 which requires only modest consumer-grade hardware \u2014 now surpasses gpt-oss-120b (a model 13\u00d7 its size) on MMLU-Pro (82.5 vs. 80.8), GPQA Diamond (81.7 vs. 80.1), and multilingual benchmarks. \u2713 Source: Qwen 3.5 9B Review \u2014 emelia.io 1.3 The Cost Arithmetic \ud83d\udca1 Self-Hosting Cost Reality Check (Verified Data) Cloud API (GPT-5.4 Mini): $0.75/M input tokens + $3.00/M output tokens (OpenAI pricing page) Break-even threshold: local deployment becomes economically advantageous above ~50M tokens/month (CMU study cited by CheckThat.ai) Electricity for Mac Studio under full load: approximately $15/month (DEV Community benchmark, March 2026) Hardware (RTX 4090): ~$1,500\u20132,000 amortized over 3 years \u2014 cost-effective for high-volume teams above the 50M token/month threshold Important caveat: below 50M tokens/month, cloud APIs typically remain more cost-effective when accounting for ops overhead \u2713 Source: Ollama Pricing 2026 \u2014 CheckThat.ai Runtime Architecture: Ollama vs vLLM vs llama.cpp vs LM Studio The inference runtime is the most consequential technical decision in a local AI deployment. Each tool optimizes for a different point on the ease-of-use vs. throughput vs. hardware flexibility spectrum. Our Best Mini PCs for Running Local LLMs (2026 Guide) covered the hardware layer in detail. Here we compare the software stack on top of it. Runtime Core Architecture Best Use Case GPU Required CPU Fallback License Ollama Go wrapper around llama.cpp + OpenAI-compat API Local dev, prototyping, single-user Optional (auto-detect) Yes MIT vLLM PagedAttention + continuous batching, Python Multi-user production API serving Required (NVIDIA CUDA) No Apache 2.0 llama.cpp C/C++ inference, GGUF format, multi-backend Edge, CPU-only, max hardware control Optional Yes (GGUF) MIT LM Studio llama.cpp GUI + HuggingFace model browser Model evaluation, non-technical users Optional Yes Proprietary (free) SGLang vLLM-style engine + structured output layer Structured outputs, multi-step agents Required No Apache 2.0 LocalAI OpenAI-compat middleware, multi-backend Multimodal, enterprise middleware Backend-dependent Yes MIT Ollama \u2014 The Default Starting Point Ollama handles model download, quantization selection, GPU memory management, and exposes an OpenAI-compatible HTTP API on port 11434 in a single command. Its model library covers Llama, Mistral, Qwen, Gemma, DeepSeek-R1, and gpt-oss, among many others. The Llama 3.1 is the most-downloaded model on Ollama\u2019s library at 111 million downloads, followed by DeepSeek-R1 at 79 million. \u2713 Source: Best Ollama Models 2026 \u2014 StudyHUB Critical performance note: Ollama caps at 4 parallel requests by default \u2014 it is not architected for high concurrency. The SitePoint benchmark (2026) confirmed that Ollama\u2019s throughput remains flat under concurrent load while vLLM scales linearly. For individual developers and small teams, Ollama is the right choice. For shared team APIs serving multiple concurrent users, vLLM is required. A documented security incident in January 2026 is worth noting: a report identified 175,000 exposed Ollama server instances accessible on public internet, with individual victims losing $46,000\u2013$100,000 per day due to unauthorized cryptocurrency mining on exposed GPU resources. Ollama does not include authentication by default \u2014 teams running Ollama in shared network environments must add a reverse proxy with authentication. Ollama\u2019s own documentation states it is designed for local-only deployment. \u2713 Source: CheckThat.ai \u2014 Ollama Pricing & Security 2026 vLLM \u2014 Production-Grade Throughput vLLM \u2018s core innovation is PagedAttention \u2014 a memory management algorithm that treats GPU VRAM like virtual memory in an operating system, dividing the KV cache into fixed-size pages rather than pre-allocating contiguous memory per request. According to Red Hat\u2019s independently conducted benchmark (published August 2025): vLLM achieved 793 tokens per second (TPS) compared to Ollama\u2019s 41 TPS at equivalent configurations \u2014 nearly 20\u00d7 higher throughput under concurrent load. At 50+ concurrent users, Ollama hit a performance cliff and request success rate degraded; vLLM maintained linear scaling. \u2713 Source: Red Hat Developer \u2014 Ollama vs vLLM Deep Dive Benchmark Important vLLM limitation: vLLM requires a CUDA-capable NVIDIA GPU . It does not support CPU-only inference and does not support Apple Silicon (Metal/MPS). Teams running on Apple hardware or CPU-only servers must use Ollama, llama.cpp, or LM Studio. llama.cpp \u2014 The Foundation Layer llama.cpp is the C++ inference engine that powers Ollama and LM Studio. As of late March 2026, the project has surpassed 99,600 GitHub stars (per the GitHub repository container package data, March 28, 2026) and releases multiple updates per week. Running it directly gives engineers precise control over quantization levels, hardware backends (CUDA, Metal, OpenCL, Vulkan, CPU), and deployment targets. Its GGUF format is the standard for distributing quantized models across the ecosystem. \u2713 Source: llama.cpp GitHub Repository \u2014 ggml \u2013 org/llama.cpp llama.cpp supports multi-GPU tensor parallelism, speculative decoding, partial layer offload from VRAM to system RAM, and GGUF format conversion from Hugging Face safetensors. The most recent stable release (b8565, March 28, 2026) added improvements to Vulkan backend and web UI. Run it directly when: deploying to edge devices or Raspberry Pi, running CPU-only in a network-isolated environment, or embedding LLM inference into a custom C/C++ application. LM Studio \u2014 Visual Workflow for Model Evaluation LM Studio provides a graphical model browser, one-click HuggingFace downloads, a built-in chat interface, and an OpenAI-compatible local server on port 1234. It runs the same underlying llama.cpp inference engine as Ollama. The practical pattern: LM Studio for model evaluation and selection, Ollama for production automation . LM Studio now natively supports gpt-oss models via LM Studio\u2019s Responses API integration (per LM Studio\u2019s official model page for gpt-oss). \u2713 Source: LM Studio \u2014 gpt-oss Model Page The 2026 Open-Weight Model Landscape: What to Actually Run The following model selection guide is based on verified specifications from official model cards on HuggingFace, Ollama\u2019s library, and LM Studio\u2019s model catalog. Hardware requirements are calculated from the standard formula: (parameters \u00d7 bits per weight) \u00f7 8 = approximate GB required . Actual VRAM usage varies depending on context length and KV cache settings. Model Parameters License Key Strength Min. RAM / VRAM (Q4) Ollama Available gpt-oss-120b 117B total / 5.1B active (MoE) Apache 2.0 Near-parity with o4-mini ~65 GB VRAM (H100/MI300X) Yes gpt-oss-20b 21B total / 3.6B active (MoE) Apache 2.0 Similar to o3-mini, 16 GB RAM ~12 GB VRAM (consumer GPU) Yes Qwen3.5-397B-A17B 397B total / 17B active (MoE) Apache 2.0 Instruction following leader (IFBench 76.5) Multi-GPU (server class) Via GGUF Qwen3.5-35B-A3B ~35B total / 3B active (MoE) Apache 2.0 Beats Qwen3-235B-A22B, 8 GB VRAM ~8 GB VRAM Yes Qwen3.5-9B 9B dense Apache 2.0 Beats gpt-oss-120b on MMLU-Pro, GPQA ~6 GB RAM (Q4) Yes Qwen 2.5 32B 32B dense Apache 2.0 83.2% MMLU, coding, RAG ~20 GB VRAM Yes DeepSeek-R1 (distilled) 7B to 70B (Qwen/Llama base) MIT Chain-of-thought reasoning 4 GB (7B) to 40 GB (70B) RAM Yes DeepSeek-R1 (full) 671B total (MoE) MIT Comparable to OpenAI o1 on math/code ~80 GB VRAM minimum Via GGUF Phi-4 (14B) 14B dense MIT Math reasoning, 80.4% MATH-500 ~9 GB RAM (Q4) Yes Phi-4-mini (3.8B) 3.8B dense MIT Edge deployment, 6 GB RAM ~2.5 GB RAM (Q4) Yes Gemma 3 (4B+) 4B and larger Gemma license Multimodal (text + image) ~3 GB VRAM (4B) Yes Mistral Small 3.1 (24B) 24B dense Apache 2.0 128K context, instruction following ~15 GB VRAM Yes Breaking: OpenAI gpt-oss \u2014 First Open-Weight Model Since GPT-2 On August 5, 2025, OpenAI released gpt-oss-120b and gpt-oss-20b \u2014 the company\u2019s first open-weight models since GPT-2 in 2019. Both are licensed under Apache 2.0 with no per-user-count commercial restrictions, making them freely deployable for any commercial use. \u2713 Source: IEEE Spectrum \u2014 OpenAI\u2019s Open Model: GPT OSS Models are Open Source The key verified specifications (from the official HuggingFace model card and OpenAI model card): gpt-oss-120b : 117B total parameters, 5.1B active per token (Mixture-of-Experts), 36 transformer layers with 128 experts and 4 active experts per token. Fits on a single 80 GB GPU (NVIDIA H100 or AMD MI300X). 128K token context window. Near-parity with o4-mini on core reasoning benchmarks per OpenAI\u2019s official announcement. gpt-oss-20b : 21B total parameters, 3.6B active per token (MoE). Runs within 16 GB of memory \u2014 accessible on consumer hardware. Delivers performance similar to o3-mini on common benchmarks. Both models use MXFP4 native quantization on the MoE weights. Both models support : function calling, web search, Python code execution, structured outputs, and adjustable reasoning effort (low / medium / high). Full chain-of-thought is available. Fully fine-tunable under Apache 2.0. Important license note : Apache 2.0 imposes no limits on commercial use and includes a patent grant \u2014 more permissive than Meta\u2019s Llama license, which requires a separate agreement for services with over 700 million monthly active users. However, both gpt-oss models are governed by an additional OpenAI usage policy in addition to the Apache 2.0 license; review it at openai.com/index/gpt-oss-model-card/. \u2713 Source: OpenAI \u2014 Introducing gpt-oss (official) \u2713 Source: HuggingFace \u2014 Welcome OpenAI GPT-OSS (official HF post) Qwen3.5 \u2014 Verified Architecture and Release Facts Qwen3.5 was released by Alibaba\u2019s Qwen team in multiple waves. The official release dates (from the QwenLM GitHub repository): February 16, 2026: Qwen3.5-397B-A17B (flagship MoE model) February 24, 2026: Medium series \u2014 Qwen3.5-35B-A3B, 122B-A10B, and 27B March 2, 2026: Small series \u2014 Qwen3.5-9B, 4B, 2B, 0.8B \u2713 Source: QwenLM GitHub \u2014 Qwen3.5 Official Repository The architecture (verified from the NVIDIA NIM model card and HuggingFace model page): Qwen3.5-397B-A17B : 397B total parameters, 17B active per forward pass (MoE). Hybrid architecture combining Gated Delta Networks (linear attention) with sparse MoE. Native multimodal (text + images + video) via early-fusion training. 201 language and dialect support (up from 119 in Qwen3). Native 262K token context, extendable to ~1M with RoPE scaling. Apache 2.0 license. Qwen3.5-35B-A3B : Only 3B parameters active per token, yet benchmarks confirm it surpasses the previous-generation Qwen3-235B-A22B on core tasks. Runs on GPUs with as little as 8 GB VRAM at Q4 quantization \u2014 the practical sweet spot for self-hosting. Qwen3.5-9B : Verified to outperform gpt-oss-120b (13\u00d7 more total parameters) on MMLU-Pro (82.5 vs. 80.8), GPQA Diamond (81.7 vs. 80.1), and multilingual MMMLU (81.2 vs. 78.2). On instruction following: Qwen3.5 leads all models on IFBench (76.5), ahead of GPT-5.2 (75.4). On agentic tool use: Qwen3.5-122B-A10B scores 72.2 on BFCL-V4, outperforming GPT-5 mini (55.5) by approximately 30%. These figures come from Alibaba\u2019s published benchmarks on the Qwen3.5-397B-A17B HuggingFace model card, which notes that empty cells represent scores not yet evaluated. \u2713 Source: HuggingFace \u2014 Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B Model Card DeepSeek-R1 \u2014 MIT-Licensed Reasoning Models DeepSeek-R1 (January 2025, with distilled updates including R1-0528 in mid-2025) is a chain-of-thought reasoning model trained via large-scale reinforcement learning. The official GitHub repository states it achieves performance comparable to OpenAI-o1 across math, code, and reasoning tasks. It is licensed under the MIT License and explicitly permits commercial use and derivative works. The practical local deployment path is through distilled variants : DeepSeek released six dense distilled models ranging from 1.5B to 70B parameters, fine-tuned from Qwen 2.5 and Llama base models using reasoning data generated by the full DeepSeek-R1. The distilled Qwen-32B variant outperforms OpenAI o1-mini on several benchmarks according to DeepSeek\u2019s published evaluations. The full 671B model requires ~80 GB VRAM minimum for reasonable inference speed. Context window: DeepSeek-R1\u2019s full model has a context length of 163,840 tokens (confirmed in the llama.cpp GGUF metadata). The maximum generation length for benchmarking is set to 32,768 tokens. \u2713 Source: DeepSeek-AI \u2014 DeepSeek-R1 GitHub (official) \u2713 Source: Ollama Library \u2014 deepseek-r1 Hardware Requirements: VRAM, RAM, and Quantization The fundamental constraint of local AI is memory bandwidth, not raw compute . A model that fits entirely in VRAM (GPU) or unified memory (Apple Silicon) runs at full hardware speed. A model that partially spills into system RAM runs at 10\u201350% of that speed depending on the PCIe bandwidth. A model that spills to NVMe storage is unusable for interactive inference. The VRAM calculation: (number of parameters \u00d7 bits per weight) \u00f7 8 = approximate GB required . A 7B model at 4-bit quantization (Q4) requires approximately 4\u20135 GB. A 32B model at Q4 requires approximately 20\u201324 GB. For a complete hardware analysis covering NPUs, FPGAs, and cluster configurations, see our Edge AI Hardware 2026: On-Device Intelligence, Architecture & Chip Comparison . Hardware VRAM / Unified Memory Practical Model Limit (Q4) Realistic Speed Notes NVIDIA H100 (80 GB) 80 GB HBM3 gpt-oss-120b at full MXFP4 Fast (>200 tok/s) Required for gpt-oss-120b single-GPU NVIDIA RTX 4090 (24 GB) 24 GB GDDR6X Qwen3.5-35B-A3B, gpt-oss-20b ~60\u2013100 tok/s (model-dependent) Sweet spot for single-user production NVIDIA RTX 4080 (16 GB) 16 GB GDDR6X Qwen 2.5 14B, Phi-4, gpt-oss-20b ~50\u201380 tok/s gpt-oss-20b requires 16 GB (verified) Apple Mac Studio M4 Ultra 192 GB Unified Very large models at Q4/Q8 ~30+ tok/s (70B) No CUDA \u2014 use Metal backend Apple Mac Mini M4 Pro 24\u201364 GB Unified 32B\u201370B models at Q4 ~25\u201345 tok/s (32B) Best consumer price/performance for Apple CPU only (64 GB RAM) N/A (system RAM) 7B\u201313B (slow) 5\u201315 tok/s Usable for low-volume offline tasks Consumer laptop (16 GB RAM) N/A or small iGPU 7B Q4 only 2\u201310 tok/s Limited but functional for light use 4.1 Quantization: Verified Quality vs. Size Trade-offs Quantization reduces model precision from 16-bit (FP16/BF16) to lower bit representations. The DEV Community benchmark (March 2026) reports that GGUF quantization methods reduced model sizes by up to 70% with less than 2% quality degradation on most tasks \u2014 consistent with the quantization research originally cited by the llama.cpp project. Specific formats verified in use (from llama.cpp documentation and model card conventions): Q4_K_M : 4-bit quantization with medium K-quant. The default recommendation for most local deployments. Approximately 75% size reduction from FP16 with minimal quality loss on standard benchmarks. Q8_0 : 8-bit quantization. Closer to original precision at approximately 50% size reduction from FP16. Preferred when VRAM is sufficient. MXFP4 : Used by gpt-oss models. Applied only to the MoE weights per the official HuggingFace model card. Enables gpt-oss-120b to fit in a single 80 GB H100. Q2_K : 2-bit quantization. Significant size reduction but measurable quality degradation on complex reasoning tasks. Useful for hardware-constrained edge deployments where approximate results are acceptable. Benchmark Comparison: Open-Weight vs. Proprietary Models The following benchmark data is sourced directly from official model cards and the publicly available evaluations cited on Qwen\u2019s and DeepSeek\u2019s repositories. Cells marked \u2018\u2014\u2019 indicate data not reported by the model\u2019s authors at time of publication, not a failure to perform. Proprietary model scores are included for reference context, sourced from their respective official release documentation. Model MMLU IFBench (Instruction) GPQA Diamond BFCL-V4 (Tool Use) License Qwen3.5-397B-A17B \u2014 76.5 \u2605 (best open) \u2014 \u2014 Apache 2.0 Qwen3.5-122B-A10B \u2014 \u2014 \u2014 72.2 \u2605 (vs GPT-5mini 55.5) Apache 2.0 Qwen3.5-9B MMLU-Pro: 82.5 \u2014 81.7 \u2014 Apache 2.0 Qwen 2.5 32B 83.2% \u2014 \u2014 \u2014 Apache 2.0 gpt-oss-120b MMLU-Pro: 80.8 \u2014 80.1 \u2014 Apache 2.0 DeepSeek-R1 (full) Comparable to o1 \u2014 \u2014 \u2014 MIT DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B Exceeds o1-mini \u2014 \u2014 \u2014 MIT Phi-4 (14B) ~78% \u2014 \u2014 \u2014 MIT \u2014 Proprietary Reference \u2014 \u2014 \u2014 \u2014 \u2014 \u2014 GPT-5.2 (OpenAI, closed) \u2014 75.4 \u2014 \u2014 Proprietary Claude Opus 4.6 (Anthropic) \u2014 58.0 \u2014 \u2014 Proprietary The pattern that emerges from verified data: open-weight models have reached or exceeded proprietary models on specific task categories . Qwen3.5 leads on instruction following and multilingual performance. gpt-oss-20b provides o3-mini-class reasoning on consumer hardware under Apache 2.0. DeepSeek-R1 distills deliver genuine chain-of-thought reasoning at 7B\u201332B scale. The remaining gap is in complex agentic coding workflows, where Claude Opus 4.6 maintains a documented advantage. Production Deployment Architecture Patterns Stage 1: Local Development (Ollama + Open WebUI) The fastest validated path from zero to a working ChatGPT-style interface on local hardware: install Ollama, pull a model, and run Open WebUI as a browser-based frontend. Open WebUI supports model switching, conversation history, and document upload for RAG. It is a one-Docker-container deployment. The Ollama CLI command ollama run [model-name] downloads, configures, and starts inference in a single step. No Python environment, no CUDA version management, no dependency conflicts. Stage 2: Shared Team API (vLLM + Reverse Proxy) When multiple concurrent users or applications need access to the same model, the architecture shifts to a shared vLLM server behind a reverse proxy. vLLM exposes an OpenAI-compatible API endpoint \u2014 the OpenAI Python SDK migrates with a one-line change to the base_url parameter. Add an API key middleware layer (nginx or Caddy) for authentication. vLLM\u2019s continuous batching handles concurrent requests without the performance cliff Ollama exhibits at 4+ simultaneous connections. For teams connecting local models into broader agentic workflows, the orchestration layer connects directly \u2014 see our Best Agentic AI Frameworks 2026: LangGraph vs CrewAI for the orchestration patterns that work with locally-served OpenAI-compatible endpoints. Stage 3: RAG Pipeline with Local Inference The dominant production architecture for local AI combines a self-hosted LLM with a vector database for retrieval-augmented generation. A verified working stack: vLLM for inference, ChromaDB or Weaviate for vector storage, nomic-embed-text (available via Ollama) for embedding generation, and LangChain or LlamaIndex as the orchestration layer. This architecture is especially effective for compliance-sensitive document search where data residency requirements prohibit cloud API use. Stage 4: Fine-Tuning for Domain Adaptation When a base model needs specialization for a proprietary domain, LoRA and QLoRA are the standard approaches. Tools like Unsloth and Axolotl manage the training workflow. For commercial fine-tuned deployments, license implications matter: Apache 2.0 models (Qwen series, gpt-oss) and MIT models (DeepSeek-R1, Phi-4) permit fine-tuned derivatives with no additional restrictions. Meta\u2019s Llama license permits derivative models but has the 700M MAU commercial threshold. Always verify the specific model variant\u2019s license \u2014 some Qwen 2.5 large variants use a research license with commercial restrictions, per the IEEE Spectrum analysis of the gpt-oss release. \u2713 Source: IEEE Spectrum \u2014 Apache 2.0 vs other open model licenses analysis Practical Recommendations by Profile \ud83d\udee0\ufe0f For Individual Developers Start: ollama run phi4-mini (3.8B, ~6 GB RAM, MIT license) \u2014 working in under 5 minutes on any modern hardware Coding: gpt-oss-20b (16 GB RAM, Apache 2.0) or Qwen3.5-9B (6 GB RAM, Apache 2.0) \u2014 both verified above GPT-OSS-120B on several coding benchmarks IDE integration: Continue extension (VS Code / JetBrains) has native Ollama backend support Evaluation tool: LM Studio for comparing models side-by-side before committing to a deployment configuration Important: Ollama is not authenticated by default. Do not expose port 11434 on any shared or public network without a reverse proxy. \ud83c\udfe2 For Enterprise / Compliance-Driven Teams Runtime: vLLM behind nginx with API key auth for shared team access; Ollama for individual developer workstations License selection: Apache 2.0 (Qwen3.5 series, gpt-oss) for cleanest commercial deployment; MIT (DeepSeek-R1, Phi-4) also fully permissive Compliance fit: Air-gapped llama.cpp deployment (no external calls, no telemetry, deterministic from seed) for highest compliance posture Hardware minimum for production use: RTX 4090 (24 GB) for single-user; 2\u00d7A100 for shared team API on 30B+ models Do not deploy gpt-oss or any model on shared network infrastructure without reviewing the model\u2019s usage policy \u2014 Apache 2.0 plus additional OpenAI usage policy applies to gpt-oss. \u2699\ufe0f For ML / Platform Engineers Default quantization: Q4_K_M for GGUF deployments \u2014 verified <2% quality degradation on most tasks at 75% size reduction Production throughput: vLLM PagedAttention delivers 793 TPS vs Ollama\u2019s 41 TPS in published benchmarks \u2014 the architecture choice matters at scale API compatibility: Design your inference layer as OpenAI-compatible from day one; every major local runtime (Ollama, vLLM, llama.cpp server, LM Studio) supports the same API surface Benchmark your own workload: published benchmark scores on MMLU and GPQA do not always correlate with performance on domain-specific tasks Primary Sources & External References OpenAI \u2014 Introducing gpt-oss (official announcement) \u2014 primary source for gpt-oss model specifications and performance claims OpenAI \u2014 gpt-oss Model Card (safety and architecture details) \u2014 official model card with Preparedness Framework evaluation HuggingFace \u2014 Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B Model Card \u2014 official benchmark table and architecture specification DeepSeek-AI \u2014 DeepSeek-R1 GitHub Repository \u2014 official release notes, license terms, and distilled model specifications Red Hat Developer \u2014 Ollama vs vLLM Deep Dive Benchmark \u2014 peer-reviewed benchmark: 793 TPS (vLLM) vs 41 TPS (Ollama) under concurrent load IEEE Spectrum \u2014 OpenAI\u2019s Open Model: GPT OSS Models \u2014 analysis of Apache 2.0 license implications vs Llama and Qwen licenses DEV Community \u2014 Local AI in 2026: Ollama Benchmarks \u2014 Ollama 52M downloads figure, MMLU benchmarks on local hardware (March 27, 2026) llama.cpp GitHub \u2014 ggml-org/llama.cpp \u2014 runtime source, star count reference, and GGUF format documentation More from NeuralCoreTech This article is part of our AI Hardware & Infrastructure coverage. Related articles: The Best Mini PCs for Running Local LLMs (2026 Guide) \u2014 hardware comparison: Mac Studio M4 Ultra, ASUS ROG NUC 15, Geekom A8 and more DeepSeek-V3 vs Llama 4 Benchmarks: 2026 Local AI Logic War \u2014 the local model benchmark comparison that put NeuralCoreTech on the map Edge & Personal AI Hardware: Building High-Performance Clusters \u2014 on-premises cluster architecture for AI workloads Edge AI Hardware 2026: On-Device Intelligence, Architecture & Chip Comparison \u2014 chip-level analysis of NVIDIA Vera Rubin, Apple M4, Qualcomm AI 100 AI for Software Engineers 2026: Claude Code, Cursor, Devin & the Complete Stack \u2014 how engineering teams integrate local and cloud models into development workflows Best Agentic AI Frameworks 2026: LangGraph vs CrewAI \u2014 orchestration frameworks that connect to local LLM backends (406 views \u2014 site\u2019s most popular) About NeuralCoreTech NeuralCoreTech covers AI tools, hardware, agents, and practical guides for developers and enterprises. All data in this article is drawn from verifiable public sources, cited inline. Subscribe to Newsletter \u00b7 AI Hardware & Infrastructure \u2192 You Might Also Like Best Hardware for AI Agents 2026: RTX 5090 vs Mac Studio vs DGX Spark Beyond Context Windows: Rethinking Persistent Memory for Large Language Model Systems Compute & Infrastructure: Investments & The AI-Driven Revolution GPU Clusters Architecture: Engineering AI Cloud Infrastructure Email I accept the privacy policy Have any thoughts? Share your reaction or leave a quick response \u2014 we\u2019d love to hear what you think! 4 0 0 #AI agents #AI hardware #Apple Mac Mini M4 Pro #DeepSeek- R1 #Edge & Personal AI Hardware #LM Studio #Local AI Server #NVIDIA #Ollama #OpenAI #Qwen3.5 #Self-Hosted LLMs #SGLang #vLLM 4 Facebook Twitter Pinterest Linkedin Reddit Email Loucas Protopappas Founder at NeuralCoreTech | AI Research & Technology Insights Platform | Zero-Trust AI, LLM Security & AI Governance | Collaborator at International Hellenic University (IHU) previous post HBM4 vs HBM3e: AI Benchmarks & Memory Performance (2026) next post AI Strategy for Small Businesses (2026): Best Tools, ROI & Step-by-Step Guide You may also like MCP Plugins: The Future of AI Developer Workflows in 2026 30 August 2026 Local AI Coding Agents: Muse Glimmer & Model-Neutral Stack 17 August 2026 Beyond Context Windows: Rethinking Persistent Memory for Large Language Model Systems 29 July 2026 Why Model Context Protocol (MCP) Is Becoming the Foundation of Secure Agentic... 22 July 2026 Kimi K3 vs Claude & GPT: Is Moonshot AI\u2019s 2.8T Open-Weight Model... 18 July 2026 NVIDIA Blackw